prompt:
Use Three.js to implement an A* pathfinding demonstration on a 3D grid terrain. Load the Fox.glb model (with walking/running animations), and have the fox automatically find and follow a path to the target cell after it is clicked.

[Three.js Import Method (Must Be Followed Strictly)]
Use the following importmap in <head>, and import via ES Modules within <script type="module">:
<script type="importmap">
{ "imports": {
    "three": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.170.0/build/three.module.js",
    "three/addons/": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.170.0/examples/jsm/"
} }
</script>
<script type="module">
import * as THREE from 'three';
import { GLTFLoader } from 'three/addons/loaders/GLTFLoader.js';
import { OrbitControls } from 'three/addons/controls/OrbitControls.js';
// Write your code here
</script>

[Model Loading]
(1) Use GLTFLoader to load assets/Fox.glb;
(2) The Fox model contains animation clips. Iterate through gltf.animations to find animations similar to Survey (idle), Walk, and Run;
(3) Use THREE.AnimationMixer to manage the animations. Play Survey/Idle while stationary and Walk while moving;
(4) Scale the model to an appropriate size (scale approximately 0.02) and place it on the cell corresponding to the grid start position (0, 0, 0);
(5) After loading is complete, set window.__3D_STATE__.modelLoaded = true;

[Grid Terrain]
(6) Create a 10x10 square grid terrain. Each cell must be a 1x0.1x1 BoxGeometry colored green (0x4CAF50);
(7) Add a small gap (0.05) between cells so they can be distinguished;
(8) Center the terrain so that the grid coordinates range from (0,0) to (9,9);
(9) Place obstacles on 15 randomly selected cells (red cubes, 0xf44336, with dimensions 0.8x0.8x0.8), while ensuring that the start cell (0,0) and the corner cell (9,9) are not blocked;
(10) Cells containing obstacles must be impassable;

[A* Pathfinding Algorithm]
(11) Implement the A* pathfinding algorithm (supporting movement in 4 directions: up, down, left, and right);
(12) Use Manhattan distance as the heuristic function;
(13) After a path is found, highlight the path cells on the terrain in blue (0x2196F3);
(14) If the target is unreachable, display an "Unreachable" message;

[Fox Movement]
(15) After a path is found, move the fox along the path one cell at a time, with each cell movement taking 0.5 seconds;
(16) Play the Walk animation while moving, and switch back to Idle/Survey after reaching the destination;
(17) The fox must face the direction of movement (turn automatically);
(18) Remove the path highlighting after the fox reaches the destination;

[Interaction]
(19) Use Raycaster to detect mouse clicks on ground cells; clicking sets the target;
(20) Highlight the target cell in yellow (0xFFEB3B);
(21) If a new target is clicked while the fox is moving, cancel the current path and calculate a new path;
(22) Use OrbitControls to allow adjustment of the viewing angle (default to a top-down view);

[HUD Display]
(23) Display the following in a HUD in the upper-left corner:
  - The fox's current cell coordinates (id="foxPos")
  - The target cell coordinates (id="targetPos")
  - Path length (id="pathLength")
  - Animation state (id="animState")
  - Number of obstacles (id="obstacleCount")

[Visual Effects]
(24) Set the scene background to light gray (0xf0f0f0);
(25) Use a DirectionalLight (with shadows) and an AmbientLight;
(26) The fox and obstacles must cast shadows onto the ground;

[State Exposure (Required)]
Update window.__3D_STATE__ every frame:
  - modelLoaded: boolean (whether the model has finished loading)
  - foxPosition: { col: number, row: number } (the cell currently occupied by the fox)
  - targetPosition: { col: number, row: number } | null (the target cell)
  - pathLength: number (the current path length; 0 when there is no path)
  - animState: string (the current animation state, "idle"/"walk")
  - obstacleCount: number (the number of obstacles)
  - isMoving: boolean (whether the fox is currently moving)
  - gridSize: number (the grid size, which must be 10)

Output a single HTML file. Place this file in the same directory as the assets/ folder and run it through a local HTTP server.
